[26.623341, "o", "001: # Review protocol — letter to opposing counsel\r\n002: \r\n003: Governs every line in `letter_lines.csv`, weighed against `original_instruction.md` and\r\n004: `clarification.md`.\r\n005: \r\n006: ## RP-401 Which record prevails\r\n007: \r\n008: On any subject the clarification speaks to, the clarification prevails. If the original instruction and the clarification take different positions on a subject, the clarification wins and the original falls away, no matter which entry the draft cites or how plainly the original was put: what the client said last is what the client instructs. For a line on a subject the clarification speaks to, the governing entry is always the clarification entry and never the original one (`RP-401`)."]
[26.623746, "o", "\r\n009: \r\n010: ## RP-402 Subjects the clarification repeats\r\n011: \r\n"]
[26.62401, "o", "012: If the clarification repeats a subject in the very terms the original used, the clarification entry nonetheless governs the line, and the line is weighed against it (`RP-402`).\r\n013: \r\n014: ## RP-403 Subjects only the original speaks to\r\n015: \r\n016: If the clarification leaves a subject alone, the original entry on that subject governs and the line is weighed against it (`RP-403`).\r\n017: \r\n018: ## RP-404 Subjects neither record speaks to\r\n019: \r\n020: If neither record speaks to a subject, nothing governs: the line's verdict is NOT_IN_RECORD and its record entry is `NONE`, however reasonable the line may read (`RP-404`). A cited_entry that exists nowhere does not by itself make a line NOT_IN_RECORD — the governing entry is determined by the line's subject under RP-401 and RP-403, not by the cited_entry. NOT_IN_RECORD applies only when the subject itself is one that neither the original instruction nor the clarification addresses.\r\n021: \r\n"]
[26.624255, "o", "022: ## RP-405 How a line is weighed\r\n023: \r\n024: Weighed against its governing entry, a line is VERIFIED when the position it takes is the entry's own, and AT_ODDS when the entry's position differs from it — the reverse, something narrower, or a different person (`RP-405`).\r\n025: \r\n026: ## RP-406 The review table\r\n"]
[26.624565, "o", "027: \r\n028: `record_entry` holds the id of the entry that governs the line, spelled as the records spell it, or `NONE` (`RP-406`).\r\n029: \r\n030: \r\n"]
[26.624801, "o", "031: ## RP-407 Cross-subject citation exception\r\n032: \r\n033: Notwithstanding RP-401, when a line's `cited_entry` is a clarification entry (CL-…) that speaks to a subject different from the line's own `subject`, the original instruction entry for the line's `subject` governs — not the clarification entry for the line's subject. The line is weighed against that original instruction entry, and the `record_entry` column reports the original instruction entry, not the clarification. (RP-407)\r\n034: \r\n035: This applies only when the cited_entry is a clarification entry for a *different* subject. When the cited_entry is a clarification entry for the *same* subject, RP-401 applies normally. When the cited_entry is an original entry or NONE, RP-401 and RP-403 apply as before.\r\n036: \r\n037: ## RP-408 Compound positions\r\n038: \r\n039: When a governing entry's position has two or more required limbs (for example, a marking and a tone requirement), a line that asserts only one limb and drops the others takes something nar"]
[26.625055, "o", "rower than the entry's own position, and is AT_ODDS (`RP-408`). The line is VERIFIED only when it takes the entry's full position, including every required limb.\r\n040: \r\n041: ## Figures\r\n042: \r\n043: `verified_count` is the number of lines whose verdict is VERIFIED.\r\n044: `at_odds_count` is the at odds count: the lines whose verdict is AT_ODDS.\r\n045: `not_in_record_count` is the number of lines whose verdict is NOT_IN_RECORD.\r\n046: `clarification_governed_count` is the number of lines whose record entry is a clarification entry (`CL-…`).\r\n047: In the answer, `Letter lines at odds with the record` is the number of lines whose governing entry takes a position\r\n048: other than the line's.\r\n"]
[26.640301, "o", "\u001b[?2004h\u001b[01;32mappuser@efd392b3b2df\u001b[00m:\u001b[01;34m/app\u001b[00m$ "]
[27.193133, "i", "python3 - <<'PY'\nimport csv\nfrom collections import defaultdict\nrows=list(csv.DictReader(open('/app/input/letter_lines.csv', newline='')))\nprint('total rows:', len(rows))\nby=defaultdict(list)\nfor r in rows:\n    by[r['subject']].append(r)\nfor subj in sorted(by):\n    print('\\\\n###', subj, 'count', len(by[subj]))\n    print('cited:', ', '.join(sorted(set(r['cited_entry'] for r in by[subj]))))\n    print('positions:')\n    for pos in sorted(set(r['position'] for r in by[subj])):\n        print('  -', pos)\nPY\n"]
